  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and Kibana confirmed both. I haven't opened the dashboard to check that each panel shows data, and the rule hasn't run yet (its status is \"pending\").\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request. It records the response code (`response`, stored as text, not a number), the response size (`bytes`), the client IP (`clientip`), the requested `url`, the source and destination countries (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`), the browser (`agent`), the OS (`machine.os`) and the `extension` of the requested file.\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-274646bb Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-274646bb-web-logs-overview-f40305ae7c53`, 7 panels): http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-274646bb-web-logs-overview-f40305ae7c53\n- Single-number panels for total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs) and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, split by the top 5 response codes (line chart).\n- Share of each response code (pie chart).\n- Top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes (table).\n- Top 10 source countries (bar chart).\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-274646bb 5xx spike\"** (id `6564d859-e58d-451b-96cb-c9627a947d0d`)\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It counts requests with a response code from 500 to 599 over the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is more than 10.\n- I created it enabled, so it starts checking right away. It has no notification action attached, so for now alerts only appear inside Kibana.\n- Because the response code is stored as text, the rule compares codes alphabetically. That gives the right result for three-digit codes.",
